        </div>
        <div class="projects-grid">
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/modulo-estadias</span> <span class="lang">java · sql</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p1.title">Estadías Module (SIEst)</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p1.desc">Complete management of the professional internship process: vacancies by area, booking with a 15-day lock, progress tracking and automatic letters.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/database.svg" alt="" />SQL Server</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/openjdk.svg" alt="" />JSP</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/openjdk.svg" alt="" />Servlets</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.problem">problem</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p1.problem">Internship vacancies existed, but administrative staff handled them and barely promoted them: few students ever found out. Paperwork was done by hand, some advisors didn't review the reports on time and there was no traceability of the process.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.solution">solution</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p1.solution">A module where the student sees vacancies filtered by their area, books two with a 15-day lock to contact the company (released when accepted or rejected), uploads progress and report documents, and receives advisor feedback. On the administrative side, advisors are assigned with a per-professor student limit and presentation letters are generated automatically with one click.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p1.how">I was only given the problem. I went to each area involved to understand how they worked, analyzed a legacy database with hundreds of tables and logic from many developers, and developed on a legacy stack with limitations.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p1.result">The module went to production and was used to manage the following internship processes. The project was recognized as the Best Internship Project 2025 at Universidad Tecnológica de la Costa.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/modulo-comisiones</span> <span class="lang">java · sql</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p2.title">Comisiones Module (SIEst)</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p2.desc">Per diem management: expense validation with invoices, tickets and evidence, authorization form with electronic signature and CFDI upload.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/database.svg" alt="" />SQL Server</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/openjdk.svg" alt="" />JSP</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/openjdk.svg" alt="" />Servlets</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.context">context</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p2.context">When an employee has a university-approved trip, they receive per diems. To validate them and avoid deductions, they must deliver invoices, tickets, evidence photos and a signed form. This was the first thing I worked on when I was hired.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p2.how">I started with the mockup and understanding the process from scratch, because I didn't know it. Then I asked what was really needed to validate each expense and only then developed the system, facing several complications along the way.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.decisions">decisions</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p2.decisions">The initial idea was to store each commission's files in Google Drive, but that was impossible: SIEst is too old and incompatible. The alternative was sending them to a dedicated email with a clear description, so no document was lost. The form employees had to print I automated so they only download it; still, they had to print, sign and upload it, and finance had to sign and upload it again: 4 signers in total.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p2.result">The document is now signed with an electronic signature by all 4 signers: faster, equally reliable and without wasting paper or ink. Also, when expenses are recorded, the CFDI upload automatically fills billing data and distributes expenses into their categories, speeding up the whole process.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/nakawe</span> <span class="lang">nuxt · ts · supabase</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p3.title">Nakawe</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p3.desc">AI health assistant that translates medical diagnoses into clear language, with accessibility and translation into indigenous languages.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/pwa.svg" alt="" />PWA</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/typescript.svg" alt="" />TypeScript</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/supabase.svg" alt="" />Supabase</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.context">context</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p3.context">In health centers, patients wait for their turn without information, receive diagnoses in technical language they don't understand and end up abandoning treatments. 60% of patients in Mexico report not understanding medical instructions after a consultation (ENSANUT 2023). Nakawe is a PWA with a conversational assistant (LUZ) that translates medical language into clear, actionable instructions, with accessibility for people with disabilities and translation into indigenous languages.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p3.how">I built a working pilot with 3 views — patient (PWA with LUZ chat), doctor (dashboard with reports and vital signs) and triage (nursing with classification). Each patient has a complete clinical profile (chronic conditions, medications, surgeries, allergies, hospitalizations) that feeds LUZ's system prompt. The assistant translates diagnoses into plain language and generates personalized reminders. Notifications include audio in Spanish and Wixárika. Deployed via GitHub, accessible with a QR scan.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.decisions">decisions</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p3.decisions">I chose PWA for instant access without downloads. I included accessibility from the design: haptic notifications for deaf people, a TalkBack-like feature for blind people, OKLCH palette with cream and green tones for visual calm. I translated alerts into Wixárika and Nahuatl for indigenous communities. The name "Nakawe" (Huichol: mother of the earth) and the assistant "Luz" seek to build trust and closeness in a clinical context.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p3.result">It generated interest among Expociencias assistants and judges. The working prototype, deployed via GitHub, proves the technical viability of the concept.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="proj-stack" style="margin-top: 8px;">
                  <a href="https://github.com/Phyton06/Nakawe" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="chip" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;"><img src="icons/github.svg" alt="" /><span data-i18n="view.repo">View repo</span></a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/rassa-jala</span> <span class="lang">python · react · ts</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p4.title">Rassa Jala</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p4.desc">E-commerce platform for farmers in Nayarit with weekly listings, orders and Saturday delivery.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/python.svg" alt="" />Python</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/react.svg" alt="" />React Native</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/postgresql.svg" alt="" />PostgreSQL</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/typescript.svg" alt="" />TypeScript</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.context">context</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p4.context">University project for a non-profit: build a platform where farmers in Nayarit publish their products weekly and buyers place orders with Saturday delivery. I led a group of 20 students.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p4.how">I designed the PostgreSQL database with normalization up to 3NF. I created the work plan splitting the project into 6 weeks with agile methodologies, generating small tasks for groups of 3 to 4 students who rotated each week because they had little experience. The stack: Python (Django) for backend because it's a marketable technology and everyone could learn from it; React Native with Expo for mobile and React with Vite for web. I configured the repos, GitHub Actions and branch protection to avoid issues from so many students developing.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.decisions">decisions</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p4.decisions">Work organization: Monday to Wednesday development, Thursday and Friday code review and fixes to merge. I taught students how to name branches, write commit messages and do code review. I also gave a course on AI-agent development so they could move faster. Every week I evaluated progress and adjusted tasks to the team's pace.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p4.result">Working platform where farmers publish products and buyers place weekly orders. 20 students trained in agile methodologies, Git and code review.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/umi</span> <span class="lang">go · react · pg</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p5.title">UMI</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p5.desc">Ride-hailing platform for Santiago Ixcuintla, Nayarit — like Didi or Uber but with local taxi drivers, where those platforms don't work.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/go.svg" alt="" />Go</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/react.svg" alt="" />React</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/postgresql.svg" alt="" />PostgreSQL</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/react.svg" alt="" />React Native</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.context">context</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p5.context">"UMI" comes from the Wixárika "ù'miet+" (Walk, Walking). In Santiago Ixcuintla, Nayarit, Didi, Uber and InDrive don't work because local taxi drivers aren't on those platforms. UMI is the alternative: an app where people request a taxi and local drivers accept it, just like those platforms but adapted to the town's reality.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p5.how">The backend is in Go with PostgreSQL and PostGIS for geolocation — spatial queries like ST_DWithin and ST_MakePoint to find nearby drivers. OTP/JWT authentication, trip lifecycle with a state machine and real-time GPS tracking. The admin panel is a React app with Vite and Tailwind that manages drivers, passengers and memberships. For the mobile apps (drivers and passengers) I chose React Native to cover iOS and Android without rebuilding the app for each system.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.decisions">decisions</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p5.decisions">Go for the backend for its performance and native concurrency with goroutines. PostGIS because geolocation is the core of the product — finding the nearest driver in real time requires optimized spatial queries. React Native for mobile because one codebase covers both platforms. React + Tailwind for the admin because it enables a fast interface to build and maintain.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p5.result">Working backend with authentication, trips and GPS tracking. Admin panel deployed. Mobile apps in development.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="proj-stack" style="margin-top: 8px;">
                  <a href="https://github.com/Phyton06/UMIAdmin" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="chip" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;"><img src="icons/github.svg" alt="" /><span data-i18n="view.repo">View repo</span></a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/aprender-es-fantastico</span> <span class="lang">react · expo · supabase</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p6.title">Aprender es fantástico</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p6.desc">Hackathon mobile app so 5th and 6th grade kids improve math and language skills, with two game modes and adaptive challenges.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/react.svg" alt="" />React Native</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/expo.svg" alt="" />Expo Go</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/supabase.svg" alt="" />Supabase</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.context">context</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p6.context">Hackathon in Tepic, Nayarit, organized by the state's Ministry of Public Education: build a mobile app to help 5th and 6th grade kids improve their math and language grades. They gave us talks about how classes are taught and brought kids to test the apps we built. I led my team.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p6.how">Two game modes: "Space Adventure", with mini games that were math challenges (bars, charts, coordinates, addition and multiplication) supported with images and videos generated by Gemini; each level passed advances you further into space. And "Nayarit Adventure": a map of the state where the kid first unlocks their own municipality and, with points, the rest; each point shows stories (like the dragon of Santiago's hill), customs, clothing and dialects, narrated to reinforce reading and listening comprehension, with mini games per story.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.decisions">decisions</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p6.decisions">I applied what I learned in the talks: never tell the kid they lost or use discouraging messages, instead support them to try again. The MVP was functional with sounds, vibrations and colors for better immersion. The AI was left pending: per-student statistics for the teacher (strengths, weaknesses and progress), learning-style tips (visual, motor, auditory or kinesthetic), and challenges that adapt to the kid's performance, explaining the problem another way if they fail again and varying content and difficulty even if two kids are at the same level.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p6.result">Participants who tested it and the kids loved it and said it would win, but I didn't place: the team didn't know each other, no one presented well to the judges and they omitted important data. It was my first hackathon: I learned a lot, improved my ability to present an idea or product and gained confidence.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
          <article class="project-card">
            <div class="proj-head"><span class="folder">📁</span> <span class="name">~/projects/modulo-checador</span> <span class="lang">php · py</span></div>
            <div class="proj-body">
              <h3 data-i18n="p7.title">Attendance checker module</h3>
              <p data-i18n="p7.desc">Automated employee check-in/check-out tracking (administrative staff, PTC and PA) in SIEst v2, with real-time pulling from ZKTEco devices and dashboards for HR.</p>
              <div class="proj-stack">
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/angular.svg" alt="" />Angular</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/php.svg" alt="" />PHP</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/php.svg" alt="" />Flight</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/python.svg" alt="" />Python</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/postgresql.svg" alt="" />PostgreSQL</span>
                <span class="chip"><img src="icons/database.svg" alt="" />SQL Server</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <details class="proj-details">
              <summary class="cta-underline"><span class="caret">▸</span> <span data-i18n="cta">more info</span></summary>
              <div class="d-body">
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.context">context</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p7.context">First module I built in SIEst v2: the checker system tracked employee check-in/check-out — administrative staff, full-time professors (PTC) and part-time professors (PA). The previous process was manual: a central computer ran the checker software and generated Excel files every fortnight, which HR received, keyed in the data, reported absences and asked for justifications. That had to be automated.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.how">how I built it</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p7.how">First I implemented Excel file upload to the system: it read the file automatically, generated the fortnightly check tables and showed the incidents. But it still depended on someone uploading the file manually. I researched ZKTEco devices and found an official brand method, along with a Python script on GitHub. I installed that script on the central computer, configured each checker's IP address and set pulling every 10 minutes. I added fault tolerance: if power or internet went out, the script saved the last successful pull time and recovered the missing data when it came back.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.decisions">decisions</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p7.decisions">With data arriving in real time, I improved several things that weren't originally planned: diagrams, charts and KPIs so HR could see attendance status at a glance. The system automatically excludes holidays and non-working days to avoid false absences. I also implemented special schedules per employee per date — if someone has a permit or a different shift, it's configured without affecting the rest. All of this prevents HR from manually reviewing every incident to decide whether it's real or not.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="d-block d-result">
                  <div class="d-label"><span class="green" data-i18n="lbl.result">result</span></div>
                  <p class="d-text" data-i18n="p7.result">The process that used to take fortnights was solved in minutes: the employee sees their incidents in real time and can fix check errors the same day, and HR gets reports without manual upload.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </details>
          </article>
        </div>
